IMPORT prelude

-- Marriage Act 1961 (Cth) -- may I marry, whom, before whom, with what notice, and is my marriage valid? -- with the numbers,
-- forms, registering authorities and late-notice circumstances set by the Marriage Regulations 2017.
--
-- SEAT: the person marrying. Every decision is phrased as that person would ask it, on facts that person could state. Where a
-- provision addresses only an official (the celebrant, the Judge or magistrate, the prescribed authority), it is encoded as what
-- it means for the person marrying: whom they may ask, what they must produce, what the celebrant must do for them and by when,
-- and whether the marriage stands.
--
-- PROVISIONS COVERED: s 5 (minor, authorised celebrant, prescribed authority); Part II: ss 11, 12 (marriageable age and the court
-- order for a 16- or 17-year-old, which lapses after 3 months), s 13(1) (written consent dated within 3 months), s 14 with
-- Schedule 1 (whose consent), s 19 (6 months before applying again), s 21 (consents and dispensations lapse after 3 months);
-- Part III: s 23B (void marriages, the prohibited relationships worked out from the relationship); Part IV Div 1: ss 32, 39, 39F,
-- 41 (who may solemnise, and where), ss 47, 47A (refusal); Part IV Div 2: s 42 (the notice of intended marriage: 1 month to
-- 18 months; who may witness the signature; evidence of birth; evidence of divorce or death), s 42(5) with reg 72 and Schedule 3
-- (shortening the month), s 42B (the separate meeting), s 43 (any day, time, place), s 44 (witnesses), s 45 (form; the
-- certificate as evidence), s 46 (the explanation), s 48 (which failures invalidate), s 50 with regs 6 and 75 (certificates, how
-- many, 14 days, to which registering authority); Part VA: ss 88C-88E (recognition of a marriage overseas); Part VI: s 89
-- (legitimation by later marriage); Part VII: ss 94, 95, 103, 104 (offences the person marrying can commit); s 111A (no action
-- for breach of promise); s 113 (a second ceremony: to resolve doubt, or a religious ceremony with no legal effect); reg 71
-- (what each party declares).
--
-- Edition: Compilation No. 34, compilation date 28 March 2026 (includes amendments up to Act No. 4, 2026), register id
-- C2026C00129, as deposited at ../../registers/source-bundle/C1961A00012.txt. Regulations: Marriage Regulations 2017,
-- Compilation No. 3, compilation date 14 October 2024 (includes amendments up to F2024L01299), register id F2024C01004, as
-- deposited at ../../../marriage-regulations-2017/registers/source-bundle/F2017L01359.txt (retrieved 2026-10-19). Used: regs 6, 71,
-- 72, 75, Schedule 3 and Schedule 4 (Western Australia).
--
-- NOT ENCODED: marriage education grants (Part IA); the procedure for dispensing with consent and consent by a magistrate or
-- Judge (ss 15-18, 20) beyond their effect as inputs; void marriages before 1985 (s 23); the registers of ministers and celebrants,
-- registration, the charge, reviews and discipline (ss 25-39M); special notices for protected witnesses (s 42A); the particulars
-- PRESCRIBED for the notice (s 42(2)(a): an approved form, not deposited); s 47B; certificate corrections (s 51); marriages by
-- foreign diplomats in Australia (Part IV Div 3); Defence Force marriages overseas (Part V); ss 88F-88G, 90-93; offences other than
-- ss 94, 95, 103 and 104 (the celebrant's offences ss 99-101 are noted, not decided); transitional and miscellaneous provisions
-- other than ss 111A and 113; regulation-making (s 120). "Domiciled", "duress or fraud", "lawfully married" and the local law of a
-- foreign country are borrowed terms, asked as facts. The Judge's, the prescribed authority's and the Minister's satisfaction are
-- inputs.
--
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Written with the writing-l4-rules skill from the individual's seat with the 2026-10-18 defaults: the Act with
-- the regulations that set its numbers and lists; the actors the text names; a judgment the law hands to a Judge, magistrate,
-- prescribed authority or Minister asked as an input, never guessed; the Act's own definitions worked out from facts. No
-- pipeline, no coverage table, no independent test pass, no human gate. NOT for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.
--
-- Dates are written YYYYMMDD as numbers. "1 month", "3 months", "6 months" and "18 months" are counted as calendar months by
-- moving the month and keeping the day of the month. A day that does not exist in the target month (31 April) sorts after that
-- month's last day and before the first of the next. That convention is an INFERENCE: the Act does not say how a month is
-- counted, and the Acts Interpretation Act 1901 is not deposited. "Within 14 days after" is counted from the day after the event.

§ `Marriage Act 1961`

§§ `dates`

@export The year of a date written YYYYMMDD
GIVEN `d` IS A NUMBER @desc A date written YYYYMMDD
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the year of` `d` MEANS FLOOR (`d` DIVIDED BY 10000)

@export The month of a date written YYYYMMDD
GIVEN `d` IS A NUMBER @desc A date written YYYYMMDD
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the month of` `d` MEANS FLOOR ((`d` MINUS (`the year of` `d` TIMES 10000)) DIVIDED BY 100)

@export The day of the month of a date written YYYYMMDD
GIVEN `d` IS A NUMBER @desc A date written YYYYMMDD
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the day of the month of` `d` MEANS `d` MINUS (`the year of` `d` TIMES 10000) MINUS (`the month of` `d` TIMES 100)

@export The same day of the month, a number of calendar months later
GIVEN `n` IS A NUMBER @desc How many calendar months later (not negative)
      `d` IS A NUMBER @desc The starting date, written YYYYMMDD
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the date` `n` `months after` `d` MEANS
    ((`the year of` `d` PLUS FLOOR (`t` DIVIDED BY 12)) TIMES 10000) PLUS (((`t` MODULO 12) PLUS 1) TIMES 100) PLUS `the day of the month of` `d`
    WHERE
        `t` MEANS (`the month of` `d` MINUS 1) PLUS `n`

@export Is this year a leap year?
GIVEN `y` IS A NUMBER @desc A year
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `is a leap year` IF
        ((`y` MODULO 4) EQUALS 0 AND (NOT ((`y` MODULO 100) EQUALS 0)))
    OR  (`y` MODULO 400) EQUALS 0

@export How many days are in the month of this date?
GIVEN `d` IS A NUMBER @desc A date written YYYYMMDD
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the length of the month of` `d` MEANS
    IF `m` EQUALS 2
    THEN IF `is a leap year` (`the year of` `d`) THEN 29 ELSE 28
    ELSE IF `m` EQUALS 4 OR `m` EQUALS 6 OR `m` EQUALS 9 OR `m` EQUALS 11 THEN 30 ELSE 31
    WHERE
        `m` MEANS `the month of` `d`

@export The date a number of days later (at most 28 days)
GIVEN `n` IS A NUMBER @desc How many days later (0 to 28)
      `d` IS A NUMBER @desc The starting date, written YYYYMMDD
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the date` `n` `days after` `d` MEANS
    IF `dd` GREATER THAN `the length of the month of` `d`
    THEN IF `the month of` `d` EQUALS 12
         THEN ((`the year of` `d` PLUS 1) TIMES 10000) PLUS 100 PLUS (`dd` MINUS 31)
         ELSE (`the year of` `d` TIMES 10000) PLUS ((`the month of` `d` PLUS 1) TIMES 100) PLUS (`dd` MINUS `the length of the month of` `d`)
    ELSE (`the year of` `d` TIMES 10000) PLUS (`the month of` `d` TIMES 100) PLUS `dd`
    WHERE
        `dd` MEANS `the day of the month of` `d` PLUS `n`

@export How old am I, in completed years, on a day?
GIVEN `born on` IS A NUMBER @desc Your date of birth, written YYYYMMDD
      `on` IS A NUMBER @desc The day in question, written YYYYMMDD
GIVETH A NUMBER
`my age on the day` MEANS FLOOR ((`on` MINUS `born on`) DIVIDED BY 10000)

§ `Part II -- marriageable age and marriages of minors`

§§ `ss 5, 11, 12 -- marriageable age and the court order`

-- s 5(1): "minor means a person who has not attained the age of 18 years."
-- s 11: "Subject to section 12, a person is of marriageable age if the person has attained the age of 18 years."
-- s 12(1): a person who "has attained the age of 16 years but has not attained the age of 18 years may apply to a Judge or
-- magistrate in a State or Territory for an order authorising him or her to marry a particular person of marriageable age";
-- (2) if satisfied that "the circumstances of the case are so exceptional and unusual as to justify the making of the order"
-- the Judge or magistrate "may, in his or her discretion, make the order sought, but otherwise ... shall refuse"; (3) the
-- applicant is then, "in relation to his or her marriage to the other person specified in the order, but not otherwise, of
-- marriageable age"; (5) the order "ceases to have effect" if the marriage "does not take place within 3 months after the date
-- of the order".
@export Am I a minor (s 5)?
GIVEN `my age` IS A NUMBER @desc Your age in completed years
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Am I a minor` IF `my age` LESS THAN 18

@export May I apply to a Judge or magistrate for an order to marry under 18 (s 12(1))?
GIVEN `my age` IS A NUMBER @desc Your age in completed years
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May I apply for an order to marry under 18` IF `my age` AT LEAST 16 AND `my age` LESS THAN 18

@export Can the Judge or magistrate make the order (s 12(2))?
GIVEN `my age` IS A NUMBER @desc Your age in completed years
      `the Judge or magistrate is satisfied the circumstances are so exceptional and unusual as to justify the order` IS A BOOLEAN @desc The Judge's or magistrate's finding after the inquiry
      `the person I would marry is of marriageable age` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the other person is of marriageable age
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Can the court authorise my marriage under 18` IF
        `May I apply for an order to marry under 18` `my age`
    AND `the Judge or magistrate is satisfied the circumstances are so exceptional and unusual as to justify the order`
    AND `the person I would marry is of marriageable age`

@export By what date must I marry under my s 12 order (s 12(5))?
GIVEN `the order was made on` IS A NUMBER @desc The date of the order, written YYYYMMDD
GIVETH A NUMBER
`The last day my court order lets me marry` MEANS `the date` 3 `months after` `the order was made on`

@export Am I of marriageable age for this marriage (ss 11, 12(3), (5))?
GIVEN `my age` IS A NUMBER @desc Your age in completed years on the day of the marriage
      `I have a s 12 order to marry this person` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a Judge or magistrate has made an order authorising you to marry this particular person
      `the order was made on` IS A NUMBER @desc The date of the order, written YYYYMMDD (any number if there is no order)
      `we marry on` IS A NUMBER @desc The date of the marriage, written YYYYMMDD
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Am I of marriageable age` IF
        `my age` AT LEAST 18
    OR  (    `I have a s 12 order to marry this person`
         AND `my age` AT LEAST 16
         AND `we marry on` AT MOST `The last day my court order lets me marry` `the order was made on`)

@export Am I of marriageable age on a given day, without a court order (s 11)?
GIVEN `born on` IS A NUMBER @desc Your date of birth, written YYYYMMDD
      `on` IS A NUMBER @desc The day in question, written YYYYMMDD
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Am I of marriageable age on the day` IF `my age on the day` `born on` `on` AT LEAST 18

-- s 19(1): after a refusal under s 12, 15 or 16, "a further application under the same section by the same person in relation
-- to the proposed marriage shall not be considered ... within 6 months after the refusal", unless the applicant satisfies the
-- decision-maker "that there has been a substantial change in the relevant facts or circumstances".
@export May my new application be considered after a refusal (s 19)?
GIVEN `my earlier application was refused on` IS A NUMBER @desc The date of the refusal, written YYYYMMDD
      `I apply again on` IS A NUMBER @desc The date of the new application, written YYYYMMDD
      `the decision-maker is satisfied of a substantial change in the facts or circumstances` IS A BOOLEAN @desc The Judge's, magistrate's or prescribed authority's finding
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May my new application be considered` IF
        `I apply again on` AT LEAST `the date` 6 `months after` `my earlier application was refused on`
    OR  `the decision-maker is satisfied of a substantial change in the facts or circumstances`

§§ `ss 13, 14, 21 and Schedule 1 -- consent to the marriage of a minor`

-- s 13(1): where a party, "not having previously been married, is a minor, the marriage shall not be solemnised unless there is
-- produced" the consent in writing of each person whose consent is required, "duly witnessed and dated not earlier than 3 months
-- before the date on which the marriage is solemnised", or an effective consent of a magistrate or Judge in place of it, or a
-- dispensation signed by a prescribed authority. s 48(2)(f): failure to comply with s 13 does not invalidate the marriage.
-- s 21(1), (2)(a): a consent by a magistrate or Judge and a dispensation each cease to have effect "if the marriage does not take
-- place within 3 months after the date" of it.
@export Must I produce written consent to my marriage (s 13(1))?
GIVEN `my age` IS A NUMBER @desc Your age in completed years
      `I have been married before` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have previously been married
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must I produce written consent` IF
    `Am I a minor` `my age` AND (NOT `I have been married before`)

@export Is the written consent dated recently enough (s 13(1)(a)(i))?
GIVEN `the consent is dated` IS A NUMBER @desc The date on the consent, written YYYYMMDD
      `we marry on` IS A NUMBER @desc The date of the marriage, written YYYYMMDD
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is the consent dated recently enough` IF
        `the date` 3 `months after` `the consent is dated` AT LEAST `we marry on`
    AND `the consent is dated` AT MOST `we marry on`

@export Is the court's consent or the dispensation still in effect (s 21)?
GIVEN `it was given on` IS A NUMBER @desc The date of the magistrate's or Judge's consent, or of the dispensation, written YYYYMMDD
      `we marry on` IS A NUMBER @desc The date of the marriage, written YYYYMMDD
      `the person whose consent was dispensed with has notified that they do not consent` IS A BOOLEAN @desc s 21(2)(b): written notice to the celebrant before the marriage (FALSE for a magistrate's or Judge's consent)
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is the court's consent or dispensation still in effect` IF
        `we marry on` AT MOST `the date` 3 `months after` `it was given on`
    AND (NOT `the person whose consent was dispensed with has notified that they do not consent`)

-- s 14: "Before a minor may marry, consent is required from the persons specified in Schedule 1". Schedule 1: item 1, each parent
-- (if a parent is alive, no parenting order is in force and no guardian under item 3 or 4); item 2, each person with parental
-- responsibility for consent under a court order; item 3, each guardian under a State or Territory guardianship order; item 4,
-- each guardian under an Act "to the exclusion of any other person"; item 5, a guardian under an Act "in addition to" the others;
-- item 6, if "no other item of this table applies", "a prescribed authority". Where items 2 and 3 both apply, item 2 is taken
-- first: an INFERENCE (the table does not rank them).
DECLARE `Whose consent` IS ONE OF
    `each parent`
    `each person with parental responsibility for consent under the court order`
    `each guardian under the guardianship order`
    `each guardian under the Act, to the exclusion of any other person`
    `a prescribed authority`

@export Whose consent do I need to marry as a minor (s 14, Schedule 1)?
GIVEN `a court order about parental responsibility for me is in force` IS A BOOLEAN @desc A parenting order granting parental responsibility to one or more people
      `a State or Territory guardianship order about me is in force` IS A BOOLEAN @desc A guardianship order made by a court, tribunal or other body of a State or Territory
      `someone is my guardian under an Act to the exclusion of anyone else` IS A BOOLEAN @desc A statutory guardian to the exclusion of any other person
      `at least one of my parents is alive` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a parent is alive
GIVETH A `Whose consent`
DECIDE `Whose consent do I need` IS
    BRANCH
        IF `a court order about parental responsibility for me is in force` THEN `each person with parental responsibility for consent under the court order`
        IF `a State or Territory guardianship order about me is in force` THEN `each guardian under the guardianship order`
        IF `someone is my guardian under an Act to the exclusion of anyone else` THEN `each guardian under the Act, to the exclusion of any other person`
        IF `at least one of my parents is alive` THEN `each parent`
        OTHERWISE `a prescribed authority`

§ `Part III -- void marriages`

§§ `s 23B -- grounds on which a marriage is void`

-- s 23B(2): "Marriages of parties within a prohibited relationship are marriages: (a) between a person and an ancestor or
-- descendant of the person; or (b) between 2 siblings (whether of the whole blood or the half-blood)." (3): the relationship
-- "includes a relationship traced through, or to, a person who is or was an adopted child", the adoptive relationship being
-- "deemed to be or to have been the natural relationship of child and parent". (5)(a): an adoption that has been annulled or
-- has ceased still counts. (6): "ancestor ... means any person from whom the first-mentioned person is descended including a
-- parent". Cousins, aunts and uncles, nieces and nephews, step-relations and in-laws are not in the list.
DECLARE Relationship IS ONE OF
    `my parent or other ancestor`
    `my child or other descendant`
    `my sibling of the whole blood`
    `my sibling of the half blood`
    `my ancestor or descendant by adoption`
    `my sibling by adoption`
    `my first cousin`
    `my aunt or uncle or niece or nephew`
    `my step-parent or step-child`
    `my former spouse's relative`
    `no family relationship`

@export Are we within a prohibited relationship (s 23B(2)-(6))?
GIVEN `the person I would marry is` IS A Relationship @desc How the other person is related to you, counting adoptions (even ended ones) as birth relationships
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Are we within a prohibited relationship` IF
    CONSIDER `the person I would marry is`
    WHEN `my parent or other ancestor` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `my child or other descendant` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `my sibling of the whole blood` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `my sibling of the half blood` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `my ancestor or descendant by adoption` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `my sibling by adoption` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

-- s 23B(1)(d): consent "is not a real consent because: (i) it was obtained by duress or fraud; (ii) that party is mistaken as to
-- the identity of the other party or as to the nature of the ceremony performed; or (iii) that party did not understand the
-- nature and effect of the marriage ceremony".
DECLARE `Consent problem` IS ONE OF
    `none`
    `obtained by duress or fraud`
    `mistaken as to the identity of the other party`
    `mistaken as to the nature of the ceremony`
    `did not understand the nature and effect of the ceremony`

@export Is my (or my partner's) consent not a real consent (s 23B(1)(d))?
GIVEN `the problem with the consent` IS A `Consent problem` @desc The worst thing wrong with either party's consent, or none
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is the consent not a real consent` IF
    CONSIDER `the problem with the consent`
    WHEN `none` THEN FALSE
    OTHERWISE TRUE

§ `Part IV -- solemnisation of marriages in Australia`

§§ `ss 5, 32, 39, 39F, 41 -- who may marry us, and where`

-- s 5(1) "authorised celebrant", for a marriage in Australia: "(i) a minister of religion registered under Subdivision A ...;
-- (ii) a person authorised to solemnise marriages under Subdivision B ...; (iii) a marriage celebrant; or (iv) a religious
-- marriage celebrant". s 32 and s 39F: ministers and marriage celebrants "may solemnise marriages at any place in Australia".
-- s 39(1): a person who under State law "has the function of registering marriages" may solemnise marriages "in that State";
-- s 39(3)(a): other officers authorised by the Minister "at any place in Australia or only in the part or parts of Australia
-- specified". s 39DA: a religious marriage celebrant is a registered marriage celebrant who "is a minister of religion".
-- s 41: "A marriage shall be solemnised by an authorised celebrant who is physically present at the place where the marriage
-- takes place and is authorised to solemnise marriages at that place."
DECLARE Celebrant IS ONE OF
    `a minister of religion registered under Subdivision A`
    `a registered marriage celebrant`
    `a religious marriage celebrant`
    `a State registering officer under s 39(1)`
    `a State officer authorised under s 39(2)`
    `a person with no authorisation under the Act`

@export Is the person who will marry us an authorised celebrant (s 5)?
GIVEN `our celebrant` IS A Celebrant @desc Who will solemnise the marriage
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is our celebrant authorised` IF
    CONSIDER `our celebrant`
    WHEN `a person with no authorisation under the Act` THEN FALSE
    OTHERWISE TRUE

@export May this person marry us at this place (s 41)?
GIVEN `our celebrant` IS A Celebrant @desc Who will solemnise the marriage
      `the place is in the State or area of the celebrant's authority` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a State officer: whether the place is in the State or the part of Australia in the authority
      `the celebrant is physically present` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the celebrant is physically at the place (not by video)
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May this person marry us here` IF
        `the celebrant is physically present`
    AND (CONSIDER `our celebrant`
         WHEN `a minister of religion registered under Subdivision A` THEN TRUE
         WHEN `a registered marriage celebrant` THEN TRUE
         WHEN `a religious marriage celebrant` THEN TRUE
         WHEN `a State registering officer under s 39(1)` THEN `the place is in the State or area of the celebrant's authority`
         WHEN `a State officer authorised under s 39(2)` THEN `the place is in the State or area of the celebrant's authority`
         OTHERWISE FALSE)

-- s 47(1): "A minister of religion may refuse to solemnise a marriage despite anything in this Part." s 47A(1): a religious
-- marriage celebrant may refuse "if the celebrant's religious beliefs do not allow the celebrant to solemnise the marriage"; (2)
-- the section "does not limit the grounds" otherwise available. The Act gives other celebrants no ground of refusal of its own.
DECLARE `Refusal` IS ONE OF
    `yes, despite anything in Part IV`
    `yes, if their religious beliefs do not allow it`
    `the Act itself gives no ground of refusal`

@export May the person I ask refuse to marry us (ss 47, 47A)?
GIVEN `our celebrant` IS A Celebrant @desc Who you have asked to solemnise the marriage
GIVETH A `Refusal`
DECIDE `May our celebrant refuse to marry us` IS
    CONSIDER `our celebrant`
    WHEN `a minister of religion registered under Subdivision A` THEN `yes, despite anything in Part IV`
    WHEN `a religious marriage celebrant` THEN `yes, if their religious beliefs do not allow it`
    OTHERWISE `the Act itself gives no ground of refusal`

-- s 43: "A marriage may be solemnised on any day, at any time and at any place."
@export May we marry on any day, at any time and at any place (s 43)?
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May we marry on any day at any time and at any place` IF TRUE

§§ `s 42, reg 71 -- the notice of intended marriage and the declarations`

-- s 42(1)(a): the notice must have been "received by the authorised celebrant solemnising the marriage not earlier than 18
-- months before the date of the marriage and not later than 1 month before the date of the marriage".
-- s 42(5): "Despite a notice ... having been received later than 1 month before the date of the marriage, a prescribed authority
-- may authorise an authorised celebrant to solemnise a marriage if the authority is satisfied that one or more of the
-- circumstances prescribed in the regulations have been met." reg 72: "the circumstances set out in Schedule 3 are prescribed".
@export What is the earliest day we may marry after our notice is received (s 42(1)(a))?
GIVEN `the celebrant received our notice on` IS A NUMBER @desc The date the celebrant received the notice, written YYYYMMDD
GIVETH A NUMBER
`The earliest day we may marry` MEANS `the date` 1 `months after` `the celebrant received our notice on`

@export What is the last day our notice supports a marriage (s 42(1)(a))?
GIVEN `the celebrant received our notice on` IS A NUMBER @desc The date the celebrant received the notice, written YYYYMMDD
GIVETH A NUMBER
`The last day our notice is good for` MEANS `the date` 18 `months after` `the celebrant received our notice on`

-- Schedule 3: 1 employment-related or other travel commitments; 2 the binding nature of wedding or celebration arrangements, or
-- any religious consideration; 3 a medical condition of a serious nature; 4 a party's involvement in a legal proceeding; 5 error
-- by an authorised celebrant (or a person believed to be one) in the notice, with the wedding arranged within less than a month.
DECLARE `Late notice circumstance` IS ONE OF
    `employment-related or other travel commitments`
    `wedding or celebration arrangements or a religious consideration`
    `a serious medical condition`
    `a party is involved in legal proceedings`
    `an error by the celebrant in giving the notice`
    `no prescribed circumstance`

@export Can a prescribed authority let us marry with less than a month's notice (s 42(5), reg 72, Schedule 3)?
GIVEN `our reason` IS A `Late notice circumstance` @desc The reason the notice was not received a month ahead
      `the prescribed authority is satisfied the circumstance is met` IS A BOOLEAN @desc The prescribed authority's finding on the documents and explanations
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Can the month be shortened` IF
        (NOT (`our reason` EQUALS `no prescribed circumstance`))
    AND `the prescribed authority is satisfied the circumstance is met`

@export Was our notice received in time for the date we marry (s 42(1)(a), (5))?
GIVEN `the celebrant received our notice on` IS A NUMBER @desc The date the celebrant received the notice, written YYYYMMDD
      `we marry on` IS A NUMBER @desc The date of the marriage, written YYYYMMDD
      `a prescribed authority authorised a shorter time` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a prescribed authority has authorised the celebrant under s 42(5)
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Was our notice in time` IF
        `we marry on` AT MOST `The last day our notice is good for` `the celebrant received our notice on`
    AND (   `we marry on` AT LEAST `The earliest day we may marry` `the celebrant received our notice on`
         OR (`a prescribed authority authorised a shorter time` AND `the celebrant received our notice on` AT MOST `we marry on`))

-- s 42(2)(c): a party signing in Australia must sign under the observation (in person or by audio-visual link) of, in Australia,
-- "(i) an authorised celebrant; (ii) a Commissioner for Declarations ...; (iii) a justice of the peace; (iv) a barrister or
-- solicitor; (v) a medical practitioner; (vi) a member of the Australian Federal Police or the police force of a State or
-- Territory"; (d) outside Australia, of, outside Australia, "(i) an Australian Diplomatic Officer; (ii) an Australian Consular
-- Officer; (iii) a notary public; (iv) [and (v)] an employee ... authorised under ... the Consular Fees Act 1955".
DECLARE `Notice witness` IS ONE OF
    `an authorised celebrant`
    `a Commissioner for Declarations`
    `a justice of the peace`
    `a barrister or solicitor`
    `a medical practitioner`
    `a police officer`
    `an Australian Diplomatic or Consular Officer`
    `a notary public`
    `an employee authorised under the Consular Fees Act 1955`
    `anyone else`

@export May this person observe me sign the notice (s 42(2)(c), (d))?
GIVEN `the observer` IS A `Notice witness` @desc Who will watch you sign the notice
      `I sign in Australia` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you sign in Australia (the observer must then also be in Australia; otherwise both outside)
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May this person observe me sign the notice` IF
    IF `I sign in Australia`
    THEN CONSIDER `the observer`
         WHEN `an authorised celebrant` THEN TRUE
         WHEN `a Commissioner for Declarations` THEN TRUE
         WHEN `a justice of the peace` THEN TRUE
         WHEN `a barrister or solicitor` THEN TRUE
         WHEN `a medical practitioner` THEN TRUE
         WHEN `a police officer` THEN TRUE
         OTHERWISE FALSE
    ELSE CONSIDER `the observer`
         WHEN `an Australian Diplomatic or Consular Officer` THEN TRUE
         WHEN `a notary public` THEN TRUE
         WHEN `an employee authorised under the Consular Fees Act 1955` THEN TRUE
         OTHERWISE FALSE

-- s 42(1)(b): evidence of "the date and place of birth of the party": (i) an official birth certificate or extract; (iii) an
-- overseas passport; (iv) an Australian passport; (iva) prescribed identification material (none is prescribed in the deposited
-- Regulations); (v) "if it is impracticable" to obtain a certificate and the party has no current passport or prescribed material,
-- a statutory declaration by the party or a parent.
DECLARE `Birth evidence` IS ONE OF
    `an official birth certificate or extract`
    `an Australian passport`
    `an overseas passport`
    `a statutory declaration`
    `nothing`

@export Have I shown enough evidence of my date and place of birth (s 42(1)(b))?
GIVEN `what I produce` IS A `Birth evidence` @desc The document you give the celebrant
      `a birth certificate is impracticable to obtain and I have no current passport` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Needed only for a statutory declaration
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Have I shown enough evidence of my birth` IF
    CONSIDER `what I produce`
    WHEN `an official birth certificate or extract` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `an Australian passport` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `an overseas passport` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `a statutory declaration` THEN `a birth certificate is impracticable to obtain and I have no current passport`
    OTHERWISE FALSE

-- s 42(10): where a party's declaration "states that that party is a divorced person or that that party's last spouse has died",
-- the celebrant "shall not solemnise the marriage unless there is produced ... evidence of that party's divorce, or of the death".
-- reg 70(2)(c)(ii) also contemplates "evidence of the annulment of the previous marriage".
DECLARE `My status` IS ONE OF
    `never married`
    `divorced`
    `widowed`
    `my earlier marriage was annulled`

@export Must I show evidence that my earlier marriage ended (s 42(10))?
GIVEN `my conjugal status` IS A `My status` @desc What you will declare about any earlier marriage
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must I show evidence my earlier marriage ended` IF
    CONSIDER `my conjugal status`
    WHEN `divorced` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `widowed` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

-- s 42(1)(c): each party declares, in the celebrant's physical presence, "(i) the party's conjugal status; (ii) the party's belief
-- that there is no legal impediment to the marriage; and (iii) such other matters as are prescribed". reg 71: "(a) if the party is
-- 18 or older -- that matter; (b) otherwise -- the party's date of birth and that an order has been made under section 12".
DECLARE `Age declaration` IS ONE OF
    `that I am 18 or older`
    `my date of birth and that a s 12 order has been made`

@export What must I declare about my age (s 42(1)(c)(iii), reg 71)?
GIVEN `my age` IS A NUMBER @desc Your age in completed years
GIVETH A `Age declaration`
DECIDE `What must I declare about my age` IS
    IF `my age` AT LEAST 18 THEN `that I am 18 or older` ELSE `my date of birth and that a s 12 order has been made`

§§ `ss 42B, 44, 45, 46 -- the meeting, the witnesses, the form, the explanation`

-- s 44: "at least 2 persons who are, or appear to the person solemnising the marriage to be, over the age of 18 years are present
-- as witnesses."
@export Do we have enough witnesses (s 44)?
GIVEN `witnesses who are or appear to be over 18` IS A NUMBER @desc How many such witnesses are present
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Do we have enough witnesses` IF `witnesses who are or appear to be over 18` AT LEAST 2

-- s 45(1): a minister of religion may use "any form and ceremony recognised as sufficient for the purpose by the religious body or
-- organisation"; (2) for anyone else "it is sufficient if each of the parties says to the other, in the presence of the authorised
-- celebrant and the witnesses, the words: 'I call upon the persons here present to witness that I, A.B. (or C.D.), take thee, C.D.
-- (or A.B.), to be my lawful wedded wife (or husband, or spouse)'; or words to that effect." A religious marriage celebrant is a
-- minister of religion (s 39DA).
DECLARE `Form of ceremony` IS ONE OF
    `any form recognised by the celebrant's religious body`
    `each of us says the s 45(2) words or words to that effect`

@export What form of ceremony is enough (s 45)?
GIVEN `our celebrant` IS A Celebrant @desc Who will solemnise the marriage
GIVETH A `Form of ceremony`
DECIDE `What form of ceremony is enough` IS
    CONSIDER `our celebrant`
    WHEN `a minister of religion registered under Subdivision A` THEN `any form recognised by the celebrant's religious body`
    WHEN `a religious marriage celebrant` THEN `any form recognised by the celebrant's religious body`
    OTHERWISE `each of us says the s 45(2) words or words to that effect`

-- s 46(1): before solemnising, an authorised celebrant "not being a minister of religion of a recognised denomination" must say
-- the words ending "Marriage, according to law in Australia, is the union of 2 people to the exclusion of all others,
-- voluntarily entered into for life." (2): the Minister may exempt a s 39(2) officer. Only ministers of recognised denominations
-- are registered under Subdivision A (s 29(a)); a religious marriage celebrant is treated as not of a recognised denomination --
-- an INFERENCE (such a celebrant might belong to one).
@export Must our celebrant explain the nature of marriage to us (s 46)?
GIVEN `our celebrant` IS A Celebrant @desc Who will solemnise the marriage
      `the Minister has exempted the celebrant` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a s 39(2) officer only: a Minister's exemption under s 46(2)
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must our celebrant explain marriage to us` IF
    CONSIDER `our celebrant`
    WHEN `a minister of religion registered under Subdivision A` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `a person with no authorisation under the Act` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `a State officer authorised under s 39(2)` THEN NOT `the Minister has exempted the celebrant`
    OTHERWISE TRUE

§§ `s 48 -- is my marriage valid`

-- s 48(1): "a marriage solemnised otherwise than in accordance with the preceding provisions of this Division is not a valid
-- marriage." (2): not invalid by reason of failures under s 42 (notice, declaration, birth evidence, "any other requirement of
-- section 42"), "the requirements of section 44 or 46", or "the requirements of section 13". (3): not invalid because the person
-- solemnising it "was not authorised by this Act to do so, if either party ... believed that that person was lawfully authorised",
-- and then the form is sufficient if it showed "an intention on the part of each of the parties to become thereby the lawfully
-- wedded spouse of the other".
-- s 42B ("must have met separately with each party before the intended marriage", both "physically present") is one of the
-- "preceding provisions" and is NOT in the s 48(2) list. Read literally, a marriage solemnised without those meetings is not
-- valid. That is what is encoded; it is a reading of the words, not a decided point. Physical presence (s 41) is not excused
-- by s 48(3), which speaks only of authority.
@export Is my marriage valid under s 48?
GIVEN `our celebrant` IS A Celebrant @desc Who solemnised the marriage
      `the place is in the State or area of the celebrant's authority` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a State officer: whether the place is within the authority
      `the celebrant is physically present` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the celebrant was physically at the place
      `the celebrant met each of us separately and in person beforehand` IS A BOOLEAN @desc The s 42B meetings
      `a s 45 form was used` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the s 45(2) words (or a form recognised by a minister's religious body) were used
      `either of us believed the person was lawfully authorised` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Relevant only if the person was not authorised
      `the ceremony showed each of us intended to become the other's spouse` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Relevant only if the person was not authorised
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is my marriage valid` IF
        `the celebrant is physically present`
    AND `the celebrant met each of us separately and in person beforehand`
    AND (   (    `May this person marry us here` `our celebrant` `the place is in the State or area of the celebrant's authority` `the celebrant is physically present`
             AND `a s 45 form was used`)
         OR (    (NOT `May this person marry us here` `our celebrant` `the place is in the State or area of the celebrant's authority` `the celebrant is physically present`)
             AND `either of us believed the person was lawfully authorised`
             AND `the ceremony showed each of us intended to become the other's spouse`))

-- s 23B(1): a marriage "is void where: (a) either of the parties is, at the time of the marriage, lawfully married to some other
-- person; (b) the parties are within a prohibited relationship; (c) by reason of section 48 the marriage is not a valid
-- marriage; (d) the consent of either of the parties is not a real consent ...; or (e) either of the parties is not of
-- marriageable age; and not otherwise."
DECLARE `Void ground` IS ONE OF
    `not void`
    `a party is already married to someone else`
    `we are within a prohibited relationship`
    `not a valid marriage under s 48`
    `a consent is not a real consent`
    `a party is not of marriageable age`

@export Is my marriage void, and on which ground (s 23B(1))?
GIVEN `either of us is lawfully married to someone else` IS A BOOLEAN @desc At the time of the marriage
      `the person I marry is` IS A Relationship @desc How the other party is related to you
      `the marriage is valid under s 48` IS A BOOLEAN @desc The answer to `Is my marriage valid`
      `the problem with the consent` IS A `Consent problem` @desc The worst thing wrong with either party's consent, or none
      `both of us are of marriageable age` IS A BOOLEAN @desc The answer to `Am I of marriageable age` for each party
GIVETH A `Void ground`
DECIDE `Why is my marriage void` IS
    BRANCH
        IF `either of us is lawfully married to someone else` THEN `a party is already married to someone else`
        IF `Are we within a prohibited relationship` `the person I marry is` THEN `we are within a prohibited relationship`
        IF NOT `the marriage is valid under s 48` THEN `not a valid marriage under s 48`
        IF `Is the consent not a real consent` `the problem with the consent` THEN `a consent is not a real consent`
        IF NOT `both of us are of marriageable age` THEN `a party is not of marriageable age`
        OTHERWISE `not void`

@export Is my marriage void (s 23B(1))?
GIVEN `either of us is lawfully married to someone else` IS A BOOLEAN @desc At the time of the marriage
      `the person I marry is` IS A Relationship @desc How the other party is related to you
      `the marriage is valid under s 48` IS A BOOLEAN @desc The answer to `Is my marriage valid`
      `the problem with the consent` IS A `Consent problem` @desc The worst thing wrong with either party's consent, or none
      `both of us are of marriageable age` IS A BOOLEAN @desc The answer to `Am I of marriageable age` for each party
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is my marriage void` IF
    NOT (`Why is my marriage void` `either of us is lawfully married to someone else` `the person I marry is` `the marriage is valid under s 48` `the problem with the consent` `both of us are of marriageable age` EQUALS `not void`)

§§ `ss 45(3), 50, regs 6, 75 -- the certificates`

-- s 50(1): the celebrant prepares a certificate "for the purpose of issue to the parties" and "2 official certificates"; (1A) and
-- reg 75: an officer holding an office specified in Schedule 4 prepares only one. Schedule 4 item 4 (Western Australia) lists the
-- Registrar and Deputy Registrar of Births, Deaths and Marriages and named offices in the Registry.
-- s 50(2): "Immediately after the solemnisation", the celebrant, each party and "2 witnesses ... over the age of 18 years" sign
-- each certificate. (4): the celebrant hands the parties' certificate to one of them and, where 2 official certificates were
-- prepared, "within 14 days after the solemnisation of the marriage", forwards one with the notice and papers "to the
-- appropriate registering authority". reg 76: they go to the appropriate registering authority; reg 6 table names it.
@export How many official certificates will our celebrant prepare (s 50(1), (1A), reg 75)?
GIVEN `our celebrant holds an office specified in Schedule 4` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For WA: the Registrar, Deputy Registrar or a listed office in the Registry of Births, Deaths and Marriages
GIVETH A NUMBER
`How many official certificates` MEANS IF `our celebrant holds an office specified in Schedule 4` THEN 1 ELSE 2

@export How many people sign each certificate (s 50(2))?
GIVETH A NUMBER
`How many people sign each certificate` MEANS 5

@export By what date must our celebrant forward the official certificate (s 50(4)(a)(i))?
GIVEN `we married on` IS A NUMBER @desc The date of the marriage, written YYYYMMDD
GIVETH A NUMBER
`The last day to forward our certificate` MEANS `the date` 14 `days after` `we married on`

DECLARE `Place of marriage` IS ONE OF
    `New South Wales`
    `Victoria`
    `Queensland`
    `Western Australia`
    `South Australia`
    `Tasmania`
    `the Australian Capital Territory`
    `the Northern Territory`
    `Norfolk Island`
    `Christmas Island`
    `the Cocos (Keeling) Islands`

DECLARE `Registering authority` IS ONE OF
    `the Registrar of Births, Deaths and Marriages of New South Wales`
    `the Registrar of Births, Deaths and Marriages of Victoria`
    `the Registrar-General under the Births, Deaths and Marriages Registration Act 2003 (Qld)`
    `the Registrar of Births, Deaths and Marriages of Western Australia`
    `the Registrar of Births, Deaths and Marriages of South Australia`
    `the Registrar of Births, Deaths and Marriages of Tasmania`
    `the Registrar-General under the Registrar-General Act 1993 (ACT)`
    `the Registrar of Births, Deaths and Marriages of the Northern Territory`
    `the Registrar of Births, Deaths and Marriages of Norfolk Island`

@export To whom is our official certificate sent (s 50(4), reg 6)?
GIVEN `where we marry` IS A `Place of marriage` @desc The State or Territory where the marriage is solemnised
GIVETH A `Registering authority`
DECIDE `Who registers our marriage` IS
    CONSIDER `where we marry`
    WHEN `New South Wales` THEN `the Registrar of Births, Deaths and Marriages of New South Wales`
    WHEN `Victoria` THEN `the Registrar of Births, Deaths and Marriages of Victoria`
    WHEN `Queensland` THEN `the Registrar-General under the Births, Deaths and Marriages Registration Act 2003 (Qld)`
    WHEN `Western Australia` THEN `the Registrar of Births, Deaths and Marriages of Western Australia`
    WHEN `South Australia` THEN `the Registrar of Births, Deaths and Marriages of South Australia`
    WHEN `Tasmania` THEN `the Registrar of Births, Deaths and Marriages of Tasmania`
    WHEN `the Australian Capital Territory` THEN `the Registrar-General under the Registrar-General Act 1993 (ACT)`
    WHEN `the Northern Territory` THEN `the Registrar of Births, Deaths and Marriages of the Northern Territory`
    WHEN `Norfolk Island` THEN `the Registrar of Births, Deaths and Marriages of Norfolk Island`
    WHEN `Christmas Island` THEN `the Registrar of Births, Deaths and Marriages of Western Australia`
    WHEN `the Cocos (Keeling) Islands` THEN `the Registrar of Births, Deaths and Marriages of Western Australia`

-- s 45(3): the certificate "is conclusive evidence that the marriage was solemnised in accordance with" s 45; (4) not
-- "(a) where the fact that the marriage ceremony took place is in issue -- as to that fact; or (b) where the identity of a party
-- ... is in issue -- as to the identity of that party."
@export Is our certificate conclusive evidence of our ceremony's form (s 45(3), (4))?
GIVEN `whether the ceremony took place at all is in issue` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether someone disputes that there was a ceremony
      `a party's identity is in issue` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether someone disputes who married
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is our certificate conclusive of the form` IF
    (NOT `whether the ceremony took place at all is in issue`) AND (NOT `a party's identity is in issue`)

§ `Part VA -- marriages overseas`

§§ `ss 88C, 88D, 88E -- recognition in Australia`

-- s 88C(1): Part VA applies to a marriage solemnised in a foreign country where "under the local law, the marriage was, at the
-- time when it was solemnised, recognised as valid" (or, by a foreign diplomat, valid under that diplomat's law and not
-- prohibited locally). s 88D(1): such a marriage "shall be recognised in Australia as valid", but not (2) "(a) [if] either of
-- the parties was, at the time of the marriage, a party to a marriage with some other person" recognised in Australia; "(b)
-- where one of the parties was ... domiciled in Australia -- either of the parties was not of marriageable age within the meaning
-- of Part II; (c) the parties are within a prohibited relationship ...; or (d) the consent of either of the parties was not a real
-- consent"; (3) where neither party was domiciled in Australia, not "at any time while either party is under the age of 16
-- years"; (4) not "at any time while the marriage is voidable" under the local law. s 88E(1): a marriage valid under "the common
-- law rules of private international law" is also recognised, but (2) not that of a person domiciled in Australia where either
-- party was not of marriageable age. s 88D(5) (an initial marriage followed by a recognised later one) is not encoded.
@export Will my marriage overseas be recognised in Australia (ss 88C-88E)?
GIVEN `the marriage was valid under the local law when solemnised` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Under the law of the place abroad where you married (or, for a diplomat's marriage, that diplomat's law)
      `it is valid under the common law rules of private international law` IS A BOOLEAN @desc The s 88E(1) fallback, a borrowed test
      `either of us was then married to someone else, in a marriage recognised in Australia` IS A BOOLEAN @desc At the time of the marriage
      `either of us was then domiciled in Australia` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Domicile at the time of the marriage
      `both of us were then of marriageable age under Part II` IS A BOOLEAN @desc 18, or 16 or 17 with a s 12 order
      `the person I married is` IS A Relationship @desc How the other party is related to you
      `the problem with the consent` IS A `Consent problem` @desc The worst thing wrong with either party's consent, or none
      `either of us is now under 16` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Today, at the time recognition is asked
      `the marriage is now voidable under the local law` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Under the local law, today
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Will my overseas marriage be recognised` IF
        (    `the marriage was valid under the local law when solemnised`
         AND (NOT `either of us was then married to someone else, in a marriage recognised in Australia`)
         AND (NOT (`either of us was then domiciled in Australia` AND (NOT `both of us were then of marriageable age under Part II`)))
         AND (NOT `Are we within a prohibited relationship` `the person I married is`)
         AND (NOT `Is the consent not a real consent` `the problem with the consent`)
         AND (NOT ((NOT `either of us was then domiciled in Australia`) AND `either of us is now under 16`))
         AND (NOT `the marriage is now voidable under the local law`))
    OR  (    `it is valid under the common law rules of private international law`
         AND (NOT (`either of us was then domiciled in Australia` AND (NOT `both of us were then of marriageable age under Part II`))))

§ `Part VI -- legitimation`

§§ `s 89 -- legitimation by the later marriage of the parents`

-- s 89(1): "A child ... whose parents were not married to each other at the time of his or her birth but have subsequently married
-- each other ... is, by virtue of the marriage, for all purposes the legitimate child of his or her parents as from his or her
-- birth or the commencement of this Act, whichever was the later." (3): only if, at the time of the marriage, "one of the child's
-- parents was domiciled in Australia", or the marriage "took place in Australia" (or under Part V or the Marriage (Overseas) Act
-- 1955). (5): it does not affect property rights under a disposition or devolution on a death before the marriage.
@export Does our marriage make our child legitimate from birth (s 89)?
GIVEN `we were married to each other when our child was born` IS A BOOLEAN @desc At the child's birth
      `we have since married each other` IS A BOOLEAN @desc A later marriage between the child's parents
      `one of us was domiciled in Australia when we married` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Domicile at the marriage
      `we married in Australia` IS A BOOLEAN @desc The place of the marriage
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Does our marriage legitimate our child` IF
        (NOT `we were married to each other when our child was born`)
    AND `we have since married each other`
    AND (`one of us was domiciled in Australia when we married` OR `we married in Australia`)

§ `Part VII -- offences the person marrying can commit`

§§ `ss 94, 95, 103, 104`

-- s 94(1): "A person who is married shall not go through a form or ceremony of marriage with any person. Penalty: Imprisonment
-- for 5 years." (4): nor with a person known, or with reasonable grounds believed, to be married. (5): "It is not an offence ...
-- to go through a form or ceremony of marriage with that person's own spouse." (2), (3): defence of a belief that the spouse
-- was dead, with reasonable grounds; 7 years' continual absence with no reason to believe the spouse alive is sufficient proof.
@export Would going through this ceremony be bigamy (s 94)?
GIVEN `I am married` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are married at the time of the ceremony
      `the other person is my own spouse` IS A BOOLEAN @desc s 94(5)
      `I believed my spouse was dead` IS A BOOLEAN @desc s 94(2)(a)
      `there were reasonable grounds to presume my spouse dead` IS A BOOLEAN @desc s 94(2)(b) (7 years' continual absence with no reason to think them alive is enough: s 94(3))
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Would this ceremony be bigamy` IF
        `I am married`
    AND (NOT `the other person is my own spouse`)
    AND (NOT (`I believed my spouse was dead` AND `there were reasonable grounds to presume my spouse dead`))

-- s 94: 5 years. s 95(1): going through a ceremony "with a person who is not of marriageable age": 5 years. s 95(2): with a
-- minor not previously married and without the required consents: 6 months or 5 penalty units. s 103: going through a ceremony
-- "knowing that the person solemnising the marriage is not authorised" while believing the other party thinks otherwise: 6 months
-- or 5 penalty units. s 104: giving or signing a notice known to contain "a false statement or an error or [to be] defective":
-- 6 months or 5 penalty units.
DECLARE `Marriage offence` IS ONE OF
    `bigamy under s 94`
    `marrying a person not of marriageable age under s 95(1)`
    `marrying a minor without the required consent under s 95(2)`
    `marrying before a person known to be unauthorised under s 103`
    `giving a false or defective notice under s 104`

@export What is the longest I can be imprisoned for, in months?
GIVEN `the offence` IS A `Marriage offence` @desc The offence
GIVETH A NUMBER
`What is the longest I can be imprisoned for` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the offence`
    WHEN `bigamy under s 94` THEN 60
    WHEN `marrying a person not of marriageable age under s 95(1)` THEN 60
    OTHERWISE 6

@export What is the most I can be fined instead, in penalty units?
GIVEN `the offence` IS A `Marriage offence` @desc The offence
GIVETH A NUMBER
`What is the most I can be fined in penalty units` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the offence`
    WHEN `bigamy under s 94` THEN 0
    WHEN `marrying a person not of marriageable age under s 95(1)` THEN 0
    OTHERWISE 5

§ `Part IX -- miscellaneous`

§§ `s 111A -- no damages for breaking an engagement`

-- s 111A(1): "A person is not entitled to recover damages from another person by reason only of the fact that that other person has
-- failed to perform a promise, undertaking or engagement to marry". (2): gifts given in contemplation of marriage may still be
-- recovered as before.
@export Can I be sued for damages for breaking off our engagement (s 111A)?
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Can I be sued for breaking off our engagement` IF FALSE

§§ `s 113 -- a second ceremony`

-- s 113(2): persons who have gone through a ceremony may go through another under Part IV Div 2 "as if they had not previously"
-- done so where there is a doubt "(i) whether those persons are legally married to each other; (ii) where the form or ceremony of
-- marriage took place outside Australia, whether the marriage would be recognised as valid by a court in Australia; or (iii)
-- whether their marriage could be proved in legal proceedings". (3): a statutory declaration with a lawyer's certificate of the
-- doubt; (4A): failure to comply with (3) or (4) does not invalidate the marriage.
DECLARE `Doubt` IS ONE OF
    `whether we are legally married`
    `whether our overseas marriage would be recognised`
    `whether our marriage could be proved`
    `no doubt`

@export May we marry each other again under the Act because of a doubt (s 113(2))?
GIVEN `our doubt` IS A `Doubt` @desc The doubt about your earlier ceremony
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May we marry again because of a doubt` IF
    NOT (`our doubt` EQUALS `no doubt`)

-- s 113(1)(a): "Except in accordance with this section ... persons who are already legally married to each other shall not, in
-- Australia ... go through a form or ceremony of marriage with each other". (5): "Nothing in this Act shall be taken to prevent 2
-- persons who are already legally married to each other from going through a religious ceremony of marriage with each other in
-- Australia where those persons have: (a) produced to the person by whom the ceremony is to be performed a certificate of their
-- existing marriage; and (b) furnished to that person a statement in writing, signed by them and witnessed by that person" of
-- the earlier ceremony and their belief. (6): ss 42, 44, 50 and 51 "do not apply", and the officiant "shall not ... prepare or
-- issue ... any certificate of marriage under or referring to this Act" or any other document unless it describes them as
-- "already legally married to each other". s 113(1)(a) itself states no penalty for the couple.
DECLARE `Second religious ceremony` IS ONE OF
    `permitted, with no legal effect and no marriage certificate`
    `not in accordance with s 113, and still with no legal effect`

@export What does our religious ceremony after our legal marriage do (s 113(1), (5), (6))?
GIVEN `we produced our marriage certificate to the officiant` IS A BOOLEAN @desc s 113(5)(a)
      `we gave the officiant the signed and witnessed statement` IS A BOOLEAN @desc s 113(5)(b)
GIVETH A `Second religious ceremony`
DECIDE `What does our religious ceremony do` IS
    IF `we produced our marriage certificate to the officiant` AND `we gave the officiant the signed and witnessed statement`
    THEN `permitted, with no legal effect and no marriage certificate`
    ELSE `not in accordance with s 113, and still with no legal effect`

@export Does our religious ceremony after our legal marriage have any legal effect on our marriage?
GIVEN `we are already legally married to each other` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the earlier civil marriage is valid
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Does our religious ceremony change our marriage` IF
    NOT `we are already legally married to each other`

-- s 101: a person "shall not solemnise a marriage, or purport to solemnise a marriage, at a place in Australia ... unless the person
-- is authorised"; s 113(7): an unauthorised officiant does not commit that offence "by reason only of" performing a religious
-- ceremony between parties who have complied with s 113(5). s 99(6): an authorised person shall not purport to solemnise a
-- marriage between persons who say, or whom the person knows or has reason to believe, are already married to each other.
-- Each: 6 months or 5 penalty units.
@export Could the officiant of our religious ceremony be prosecuted (ss 99(6), 101, 113(7))?
GIVEN `the officiant is an authorised celebrant` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the priest or officiant is authorised under the Act
      `the officiant knew or was told we were already married` IS A BOOLEAN @desc s 99(6)
      `we produced our marriage certificate to the officiant` IS A BOOLEAN @desc s 113(5)(a)
      `we gave the officiant the signed and witnessed statement` IS A BOOLEAN @desc s 113(5)(b)
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Could our officiant be prosecuted` IF
        (NOT (`we produced our marriage certificate to the officiant` AND `we gave the officiant the signed and witnessed statement`))
    AND (   (NOT `the officiant is an authorised celebrant`)
         OR `the officiant knew or was told we were already married`)
